('77abed4e-4eea-4dee-be77-f7bf84544a54', 'President and first lady welcome Saudi crown prince back to the White House for a black-tie dinner', '<p>Saudi Arabia’s Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman returned to the White House on Tuesday evening for a black-tie dinner, capping a day of high-level diplomacy that included an&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/18/politics/khashoggi-murder-trump-bin-saudi-crown-prince\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>Oval Office meeting</strong></a>&nbsp;and bilateral lunch with President Donald Trump.</p><p>Trump, dressed in a black tuxedo, and first lady Melania Trump, wearing an emerald gown, greeted the crown prince at the South Lawn entrance as a military band stood at attention, marking a ceremonial welcome underscoring the significance of the visit.</p><p>The guest list includes largely chief executives, as well as lawmakers and governors — some of whom Trump himself called to invite to the dinner, people familiar with the plans&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/16/politics/saudi-crown-prince-trump-white-house\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>previously told CNN</strong></a>. The event is being coordinated by Melania Trump, as all state visits are planned through the first lady’s office.</p><p>While the White House has decided to pull out all the stops, the visit cannot be classified as an official state visit as Prince bin Salman is not Saudi Arabia’s head of state. That position is occupied by his 89-year-old father,&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2015/01/23/middleeast/who-is-saudi-arabia-king-salman\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>King Salman</strong></a>. But the crown prince has assumed almost all day-to-day responsibility for ruling the kingdom, and attends summits and other diplomatic engagements as the country’s leader.</p><p><br></p><p>House Oversight Chair James Comer highlighted an email Democratic fundraisers sent to Jeffrey Epstein on behalf of Hakeem Jeffries in 2013 as part of a broader accusation that Democrats were cherry-picking information in the files from the convicted sex offender’s estate to selectively criticize President Donald Trump.</p><p>“Another email shows Democrat fundraisers invited Epstein to an event or to meet privately with Hakeem Jeffries as part of their 2013 effort to win a majority. So Hakeem Jeffries’ campaign solicited money from Jeffrey Epstein. That’s what we found in the last document batch,” Comer said on the House floor on Tuesday.</p><p>A Democratic consulting group asked Epstein in a March 2013 email if he wanted to participate in a fundraising dinner with Jeffries and former President Barack Obama or get to know Jeffries better.</p><p>“Sometimes referred to as ‘Brooklyn’s Barack,’ he is a staunch supporter of President Obama and a progressive voice for the people of New York City,” the email from Dynamic SRG reads.</p><p>There is no evidence Epstein responded. CNN has reached out to Dynamic SRG for comment. A Jeffries spokesperson pointed to FEC filings, which show the consulting group has not worked with Jeffries since 2015. And there is no&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.fec.gov/data/receipts/individual-contributions/?contributor_name=Epstein%2C+Jeff&amp;two_year_transaction_period=2014\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>record</strong></a>&nbsp;of Epstein giving Jeffries money directly.</p><p class=\"ql-align-right\"><br></p><p><br></p>', 'cf5e983b-3e96-4d99-803c-94b120cbae05', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'Yes', '1763529118_8ff3f9238100c7eefd64.png', '2025-11-19 05:11:58.917928', '2025-11-19 05:12:33.270905', NULL),
('92a212ee-b5c7-404b-a5b8-e1035b414196', 'Election Center 2025', '<h1><strong>Election Center 2025</strong></h1><p>In the first major electoral test of the second Trump administration, Democrats swept the major races in Virginia, New Jersey and New York City, while voters in California approved a new congressional map that could help Democrats win five more House seats next year.</p><p>In the first major electoral test of the second Trump administration, Democrats swept the major races in Virginia, New Jersey and New York City, while voters in California approved a new congressional map that could help Democrats win five more House seats next year.</p><p>In the first major electoral test of the second Trump administration, Democrats swept the major races in Virginia, New Jersey and New York City, while voters in California approved a new congressional map that could help Democrats win five more House seats next year.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><br></p><ul><li><br></li></ul><p><br></p><p><br></p>', '147b4f6c-5a01-48d0-b45e-925d23f0612b', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'Yes', '1763529749_9b21a39177a6ed2f163e.png', '2025-11-19 05:22:29.156283', '2025-11-19 05:23:46.207715', NULL),
