('35d75642-2721-45ee-a544-1503ffb9393d', 'Democrats win across the spectrum as voters reject Trump', '<p>Voters up and down the East Coast&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/politics/live-news/election-news-11-04-25\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>delivered Democrats a sweep</strong></a>&nbsp;on Tuesday, electing candidates across the party’s ideological spectrum in a vivid show of discontent with President Donald Trump nearly a year into his second term.</p><p>In Virginia, moderate former Rep.&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/04/politics/virginia-governor-abigail-spanberger-wins\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>Abigail Spanberger</strong></a>&nbsp;turned in the strongest Democratic performance in the state’s recent history as she coasted to victory. And in New Jersey, another moderate, Rep.&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/04/politics/nj-governor-mikie-sherrill-wins\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>Mikie Sherrill</strong></a>, busted apart the coalition Trump and her Republican rival, former state lawmaker Jack Ciattarelli, had put together to close the Garden State’s gap in recent elections.</p><p>In New York City, democratic socialist&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/04/politics/nyc-mayor-zohran-mamdani-wins\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>Zohran Mamdani’s win</strong></a>&nbsp;marked the second time this year he’d defeated former Gov. Andrew Cuomo — first in the Democratic primary, and then in the general election, with Cuomo running as an independent backed by Trump.</p>', 'cf5e983b-3e96-4d99-803c-94b120cbae05', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'Yes', '1763530827_b10883a65a0555653381.png', '2025-11-19 05:40:27.672368', '2025-11-19 05:40:54.181763', NULL),
('8500bcca-2049-4e15-971e-5bdb3ab6c5fe', 'Sabiu Lawali Tsafe', '<p>Sabu</p>', '147b4f6c-5a01-48d0-b45e-925d23f0612b', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1763531025_c7f5397a3849114c21aa.jpg', '2025-11-19 05:43:45.495698', '2025-11-19 05:43:55.597094', NULL),
('dc6281cc-5b9b-4258-b223-3b7bd9ec1e78', 'TWG-GBV condemns attack on GGSS Maga, seeks urgent action', '<p>The Kebbi State Technical Working Group on Gender-Based Violence (TWG-GBV) has condemned the attack on Government Girls’ Secondary School, Maga, in the Zuru Emirate, which led to the death of a staff member, injury to a security officer and abduction of 25 female students.</p><p><br></p><p>Alhaji Nasiru Idris, Secretary of the TWG-GBV, made this known while briefing newsmen on behalf of the group on Friday in Birnin Kebbi.</p><p><br></p><p>He said the TWG-GBV, chaired by the First Lady of Kebbi State, Hajiya Zainab Nasare (Kauran Gwandu), described the incident as “tragic, unacceptable and a direct attack on the future of the Nigerian child”.</p><p><br></p><p>According to him, the Group expressed sympathy to the families, parents and the entire Zuru Emirate over the attack and abduction.</p><p><br></p><p>Idris said the TWG-GBV also acknowledged the immediate steps taken by Gov. Nasiru Idris and the First Lady, who have been mobilising security agencies and activating rescue operations to ensure the safe return of the 25 abducted students.</p><p><br></p><p>He said the Group called for urgent and coordinated action to support ongoing rescue efforts and strengthen security across schools in the state.</p><p><br></p><p>The secretary listed some of the immediate priority actions as strengthening security around schools, activating community early-warning systems, deploying specialised rescue and intelligence teams, and providing psychosocial support to affected families and students.</p><p><br></p><p>He added that the Group further recommended continuous communication with parents and guardians to reduce anxiety and curb misinformation.</p><p><br></p><p>On medium-term measures, Idris said the TWG-GBV urged government to upgrade physical and digital security infrastructure in schools, empower community vigilance groups, and consider converting the Zuru Airstrip and other strategic locations into Forward Operating Bases (FOBs) for enhanced surveillance.</p><p><br></p><p>He said the Group also advised the state to establish a Statewide Security Coordination and Response Framework to improve collaboration between security agencies, traditional rulers, faith leaders and community structures.</p><p><br></p><p>Idris said the TWG-GBV appealed to government to consider declaring a work-free day for statewide prayers and reflection in support of the safe return of the girls.</p><p><br></p><p>He urged residents to remain calm, vigilant and supportive of rescue operations, and to report any suspicious movement to relevant authorities.</p><p><br></p><p>“ The thoughts, prayers and solidarity of the TWG-GBV remain with the families of the abducted girls, the Zuru Emirate and the entire state,” the secretary said.</p>', 'cf5e983b-3e96-4d99-803c-94b120cbae05', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'Yes', '1763811717_83f3d2c618750f79f0dd.jpg', '2025-11-22 11:41:57.954845', '2025-11-22 11:45:07.512821', NULL),
('76acf6f4-2312-4bcf-a6f4-02fbb957bbea', 'Zelensky warns of ‘difficult moment in history’ after Trump gives Ukraine days to accept his plan to end war', '<h2>US President Donald Trump has given Ukraine less than a week to accept his plan – widely seen as favoring Russia – to end the war, as President Volodymyr Zelensky said his country faced “one of the most difficult moments” in its history.“I’ve had a lot of deadlines, but if things are working well, you tend to extend the deadlines. But Thursday is it,” the US president said in a radio interview with Fox News.The 28-point plan put forward by Trump&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/21/politics/trump-ukraine-peace-gaza-witkoff\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>puts pressure</strong></a>&nbsp;on Kyiv to cede territory, limit the size of its military and promise not to join NATO in exchange for an end to the war, all long-standing Kremlin demands.It was characterized by Zelensky during a video address to the nation on Friday as presenting Ukraine with a choice between losing its dignity or a key ally.</h2><h2><br></h2><h2>US President Donald Trump has given Ukraine less than a week to accept his plan – widely seen as favoring Russia – to end the war, as President Volodymyr Zelensky said his country faced “one of the most difficult moments” in its history.“I’ve had a lot of deadlines, but if things are working well, you tend to extend the deadlines. But Thursday is it,” the US president said in a radio interview with Fox News. The 28-point plan put forward by Trump&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/2025/11/21/politics/trump-ukraine-peace-gaza-witkoff\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>puts pressure</strong></a>&nbsp;on Kyiv to cede territory, limit the size of its military and promise not to join NATO in exchange for an end to the war, all long-standing Kremlin demands.It was characterized by Zelensky during a video address to the nation on Friday as presenting Ukraine with a choice between losing its dignity or a key ally.</h2><h2><br></h2><h2><br></h2><p><br></p>', 'cf5e983b-3e96-4d99-803c-94b120cbae05', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1763812333_5081c39fa0e24688ff75.jpg', '2025-11-22 11:52:13.515330', '2025-11-22 11:53:03.953211', NULL),
('805ee8be-8e80-475e-a989-9abbb89884fb', 'News Article of random text generation', '<p>Option A: Random lorem-style placeholder text</p><p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Pellentesque vitae velit id arcu placerat bibendum. Quisque at nisl vel augue efficitur bibendum. Integer non nunc nec augue facilisis tristique. Sed ac arcu ac augue posuere gravida. Phasellus a arcu nec arcu efficitur facilisis. Nam nec arcu at lorem bibendum posuere. Praesent vel nibh vitae lorem pulvinar dapibus.</p><p>Option B: Random storytelling starter (about 180 words)</p><p>The rain whispered against the old window as Mara found a folded map tucked inside the library’s forgotten atlas. It led to a place where the streetlights hummed like distant bees, and every corner turned a new color in the spectrum of memory. She followed the trail through rain-slicked sidewalks, past a bakery that only sold yesterday’s dreams, and into an alley where silence wore a velvet cloak. At the end stood a door painted the color of rain-soaked slate. On it was etched a single line: When you remember, you become part of the story. Mara hesitated, then stepped through. The room beyond smelled of iron and lilacs, and in the center of the floor rested a mirror that did not reflect the present, but possibilities—images of a life not yet breathed, a choice not yet made, a future that could still be rewritten. She took a breath and walked toward the future, one memory at a time.</p><p>Option C: Short random sentences (about 8–10 lines)</p><p>The kettle sang a curious tune at dawn. Blue bicycles glided over sunlit puddles. A notebook fluttered open to pages that smelled of cinnamon. Clouds tiptoed across the horizon like shy dancers. The clock forgot to tick for a moment, just to listen. A compass whispered directions to a place forgotten by maps. Shadows learned to smile as the city woke. Somewhere, the quiet found a mouthful of laughter.</p>', '1b65cdf5-a33b-4c92-ae8f-672f72f331d11', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1764390445_bc86594774c6f564bd55.jpg', '2025-11-29 04:27:25.127850', '2025-11-29 04:27:39.559054', NULL),
('5bb1e8dd-b4f5-4dfc-9878-cfbeb26aa721', 'How did Hong Kong’s massive housing estate fire turn so deadly? Here’s what we know', '<p><br></p><p><strong>Hong Kong&nbsp;—&nbsp;</strong>A deadly inferno torn through a massive housing complex in Hong Kong earlier this week, killing at least 128 people with many more still missing, in&nbsp;<a href=\"https://www.cnn.com/world/live-news/hong-kong-tai-po-buildings-fire-11-26-25\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"color: rgb(12, 12, 12);\"><strong>the city’s worst disaster in decades</strong></a>.</p><p>Officials said the blaze was officially put out on Friday morning local time – nearly two days after fires broke out in the towers of the Wang Fuk Court complex – with rescuers saying extremely high temperatures hampered their ability to reach trapped residents.Questions are swirling on how such a fire in a skyscraper-filled city with a usually strong public safety record and construction standards could become so deadly, leaping from building to building.</p><p>Many of the more than 4,000 people who lived in the<strong>&nbsp;</strong>public housing estate in the city’s Tai Po neighborhood were aged 65 and over. The exact cause of the fire is not yet known, but a criminal investigation has been launched. The complex was under renovation and encased in bamboo scaffolding and safety netting – a construction technique that’s ubiquitous in Hong Kong and parts of mainland China. Authorities are also investigating whether flammable material, including polystyrene boards blocking windows of multiple apartments, may have contributed to the inferno.</p><p>On Saturday, the Building Department suspended 28 other projects handled by the same construction firm across the city and halted work at two separate sites involving different contractors using a similar material. First responders initially focused their efforts on three of the seven blocks affected, where 150 people were still missing on Saturday, authorities said.</p><p>Firefighters conducted break-in operations targeting all residential units to assess if anyone remained trapped inside.The deputy director of the Hong Kong Fire Services Derek Armstrong Chan said earlier this week that firefighting efforts took “longer than expected” because the high-rise inferno was “much worse” than first thought.</p><iframe class=\"ql-video ql-align-center\" frameborder=\"0\" allowfullscreen=\"true\" src=\"https://1a884dd85feacb69ca70b516e0e0f220.safeframe.googlesyndication.com/safeframe/1-0-45/html/container.html\" height=\"250\" width=\"300\"></iframe><p class=\"ql-align-center\"><br></p><p><br></p>', 'a2034f4a-5678-4b19-b523-81732828eb1', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1764473713_6e24e8e6b5fe32779459.jpg', '2025-11-30 03:35:13.542620', '2025-11-30 03:44:03.116956', NULL),
('8f7ea2eb-67b9-4df3-930c-cb4e64fde512', 'The Noon Secret of Lumen Bay', '<p>In the quiet harbor town of Lumen Bay, a clock tower stood like a patient guardian over the water. Every noon, its brass hands clicked together in a precise whisper, and the town’s children gathered to listen for the one minute when the clock would reveal a secret. On this particular day, Mina, a curious apprentice lantern-maker, stood at the edge of the pier with a small brass key tucked into her sleeve. The tide hummed softly, a tune she felt more than heard, as if the sea kept a diary of every dream the town had forgotten.</p><p>The clock tower began its noon ritual, but instead of the usual secret, a glimmer escaped from the clock’s face—a thread of light curling downward like a silver fish. Mina followed it, coins jingling in her pocket from a neighbor’s stray bet that the harbor would yield a miracle before dusk. The thread led to an old warehouse where lanterns slept in dust, their wicks cold as winter stars.</p><p>Inside, Mina found a lantern that pulsed with faint blue heat. She fitted the brass key into its base, and the lantern woke, casting a map of constellations across the walls. The light pointed toward the sea, toward a forgotten shoreline where a lighthouse kept a vigil for a long-lost traveler. Mina realized the clock’s secret wasn’t a treasure, but a promise: to guide brave hearts back to home, one quiet noon at a time.</p>', 'a2034f4a-7504-4b19-b523-81730f828eb0', 'published', '12345abcd54321', 'No', '1764473845_37f553eb71cc2e19d4f6.jpg', '2025-11-30 03:37:25.504786', '2025-11-30 03:43:59.232176', NULL),
